Diagnose and fix a flaky test without deleting it. Save /workspace/tags.py:

def unique_tags(posts):
    tags = set()
    for p in posts:
        for t in p["tags"]:
            tags.add(t)
    return list(tags)

and /workspace/tests/test_tags.py:

from tags import unique_tags

def test_unique_tags():
    posts = [{"tags": ["python", "tdd"]}, {"tags": ["tdd", "ci"]}]
    assert unique_tags(posts) == ["python", "tdd", "ci"]

Demonstrate the flakiness empirically: run PYTHONHASHSEED=0 pytest and PYTHONHASHSEED=1 pytest (and a loop of 10 varied seeds via a shell loop) and show it passes on some seeds and fails on others, because set iteration order for strings depends on hash randomization. Then fix it properly, twice over: (1) decide the function SHOULD have a deterministic contract, change unique_tags to preserve first-seen order (dict.fromkeys) and keep the test exact; (2) add a second test that treats order as irrelevant by comparing as sets, as documentation of the alternative. Rerun the 10-seed loop and show 10/10 green. Then add a regression guard: a conftest.py assertion or comment explaining why PYTHONHASHSEED must not be pinned in CI to keep this class of bug visible.

